WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.130
PROFESSOR: So now we're
going to see if we

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.320
can apply what we just
learned about elasticity

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.450
and how to measure it
by cooking a steak.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.900
We're then going to measure the
elasticity of a raw piece of steak

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.960
and a piece of steak that is cooked
to about well or medium well,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.440
and we're going to see how
the elasticity of the steak

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.140
changes as the steak cooks.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.290
We have a steak here.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.880
And in order to do this scientifically,
we have cut out little pieces of steak

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.838
and they're all the same
dimensions, so they're

00:00:27.838 --> 00:00:29.780
four centimeters by four centimeters.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:33.890
And we're going to apply a weight to the
steaks before and after they're cooked,

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.990
and we're going to see
how much they compress.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:38.510
And then we're going to use
the equation of the week

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.850
and see if we can
calculate the elasticity.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.300
OK, so here's our little setup
for how to measure elasticity.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.000
Very simple.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.180
You can even set this
up in your own kitchen.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.490
So we have a plastic
plate that we're going

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:53.570
to place on top of the piece of
food, and then we have a ruler,

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.010
and then we have a weight.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.760
And here I'm showing
you a 200 gram weight

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.720
that we're just going to
place on top of the steak.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:05.669
And as you can see, the initial
measurement for this is 30 millimeters.

00:01:05.669 --> 00:01:07.460
And it's a little bit
hard to see, but then

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.560
as we apply the weight,
the 200 gram weight,

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.960
the measurement goes
down to 26 millimeters.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.779
So there is a compression
of four millimeters.

00:01:15.779 --> 00:01:17.570
So now what we can do
is take these numbers

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.850
and plug them into the
equation of the week

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.660
and see what we get for elasticity.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.830
So we know that elasticity
is stress over strain,

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.170
and stress is the force over the area.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.480
And we already know what the
area is because I told you

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.930
that each side of the steak is four
centimeters by four centimeters,

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.370
so that's 16 centimeters squared.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:39.560
And the force is the mass times
the gravitational constant.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.370
So the mass is 200 grams,
that's 0.2 kilograms,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.591
times 10 meters per second squared.

00:01:46.591 --> 00:01:47.090
OK.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:50.960
And for our strain, we have the
delta L, the difference that we got,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.080
which was four millimeters,
over the initial height.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.990
So that is four millimeters
over 30 millimeters.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:58.970
So for now, just plug all of this in.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.660
This is two Newtons over
16 centimeters squared,

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:08.419
and 16 centimeters squared
is 0.0016 meters squared,

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:11.790
so we need to make sure we
go back to the SI units.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:15.190
And then we divide this with
four over 30 millimeters.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:19.620
And so this turns out to be
9,400 Newtons per meter squared.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.610
So this is our elastic
modulus for a raw steak.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:27.210
Now let's go and do this for a cooked
steak, which Daniel has cooked for us.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.330
So here we now have a
cooked piece of steak

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.500
and it's been cooked
to about medium well.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.360
And the force is still the same.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.220
It's 200 grams times the gravitational
constant, so it's two newtons.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:41.450
And the area is the same, it's four
centimeters times four centimeters.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.090
And as we apply the weight,
you can see how the steak

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.860
has compressed to 28 millimeters.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.240
So there's a change and there's
a compression of two millimeters,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:54.590
so about half as much as
you saw in the raw steak.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.640
So now let's plug this
into the equation.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.370
And so this turns out to be
18,700 Newtons per meter squared.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.710
So this is about twice the elastic
modulus as we saw for the raw steak.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:11.650
So the steak has actually become
stiffer as we cooked the steak.

